{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01U4gmpMc2sk5bYuvAjAS84F","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (36.1KB). Full output saved to: /Users/leemoore/.claude/projects/-Users-leemoore-code-pi-long-horizon-liminal-context/73841117-4412-4191-bf6e-d9dac3fc51d8/tool-results/biiqhp7zi.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\npackages/lhc/test/intake.test.ts:1:// Flow 2 (recording half): TC-2.1, TC-2.8, TC-1.4 (closing Story 1's\npackages/lhc/test/intake.test.ts:93:  it(\"TC-1.4: the same batch by thread id and by file path produces identical results and read-back (closes Story 1's deferral)\", async () => {\npackages/lhc/test/threads.test.ts:2:// plus lazy-init and read-path-equivalence supplementals). TC-1.4 is owned by\npackages/lhc/test/threads.test.ts:241:  // TC-1.4 (full id/path equivalence under message-events): Story 1's named\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:249:Resolution feeds AC-1.6's id-or-path equivalence, which is implemented in exactly one place: a `resolveThreadRef` helper on the threads surface that turns `{ threadId }` into a path via the registry and passes `{ filePath }` through untouched. Every thread-scoped operation calls it first; no other code ever interprets a thread reference. The CLI's `--thread-id`/`--file-path` flags map onto the same helper, which is what makes TC-1.4's SDK/CLI equivalence structural rather than tested-into-existence.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:256:| TC-1.4 | Id and path equivalence | `resolveThreadRef` as the single entry |\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:606:**Acceptance-risk reminder:** AC-1.6 is only half-testable until `message-events` exists (TC-1.4 sends batches); the chunk proves read-path equivalence and leaves a named deferral for Chunk 2 to close.\npackages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/tech-design.md:611:**Delivers:** Flow 4